WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.160
There was an intercollege dance competition

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.820
for which a few boys and a few girls were to be selected.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.500
So this is what we have with us.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.300
Out of the ten boys and the eight girls,

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.780
three boys and three girls were to be selected.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.620
In how many ways can this be done?

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.780
Let's try and understand the question well!

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.820
There are a total of ten boys and eight girls.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.680
And out of these, we need to select three boys and three girls.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.620
The best way to understand this

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.100
is by considering the boys and the girls separately.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.780
So the two cases are: selecting three boys out of ten,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.560
and selecting three girls out of eight.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.200
In fact, separating the two cases was necessary.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.860
We cannot select boys from the group of girls or vice-versa.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.000
That doesn't make sense.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.100
Let's first look at selecting three boys out of ten.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.320
Before we begin solving any permutation and combination problem,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.520
you should know if the order matters or not!

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.700
Does the order matter here?

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.040
To know if the order matters, simply read the statement well.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.280
Selecting three out of ten.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.560
Is there any other condition?

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.860
There isn't!

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.520
Selecting B1, B2, B3 is the same as selecting B2, B1, B3

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.200
or B3, B2, B1.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.380
We are just selecting 3.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.540
And similarly,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.740
the order does not matter for selecting 3 girls out of 8.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.880
How do we solve this problem?

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.020
We haven't seen the P and C formulae yet.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.520
How do we solve this problem based on logic?

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.580
Okay, how about we solve this case first,

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.940
then this one, and then we will see what to do.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.820
So selection of three boys out of ten.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.460
We know that there are ten boys!

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.860
We can select any three boys.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.840
To show that we can say 's three times'.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.040
What about the remaining seven?

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.660
Each of them is not selected.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.140
Out of ten, three are selected and seven are not selected.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.600
And this becomes a classic case of the

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.320
anagram problems we have seen.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.900
There are 10 people.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.600
So 10 factorial will include all possibilities.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.140
Now this 10 factorial assumes that each of these 10 cases is different.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:27.640
But that's not true.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.960
These 3 are not distinct, and these 7 also are not distinct.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.880
Now how does this 10 factorial

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.780
count the cases of 3 boys being selected?

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.800
It assumes that B1, B2, B3

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.680
is different from B2, B1, B3

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.280
or B3, B2, B1 and so on...

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.880
There are three factorial

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.240
or six such ways which 10 factorial assumes to be different.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.120
But all these are the same.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.460
The order does not matter.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.640
So to eliminate the repeated cases,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.320
we divide this by 3 factorial.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:05.220
And as there are 7 boys that are not selected,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.460
we divide it again by 7 factorial.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.520
That's the answer for this part.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.620
These many ways in which we can select 3 boys out of 10.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.420
If you're not sure about how we got this,

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.100
please go through our video based on anagrams,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.820
and you will surely get a better idea.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.520
Now we can move on to the second part of selecting 3 girls out of 8.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.760
There are 8 girls in all.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.080
Using this concept that we just learnt,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.000
Can you find the number of ways

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.780
in which we can select 3 girls out of the 8.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.520
Am sure all of you got it right.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.400
As 3 girls are selected,

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.580
we write 's three times'.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.220
And the remaining 5 are not selected,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.360
so we write 'n s 5 times'.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.220
Out of these 8, these 3 are similar,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.920
and these 5 are also similar.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.640
As there are 8 girls, we write 8 factorial here,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
and this includes all possibilities

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.720
assuming each of the 8 cases are different.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.800
But we know that they are not!

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.740
For this we divide 8 factorial by 3 factorial .

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.440
And for this we divide it by 5 factorial.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.040
These many ways,in which we can select 3 girls out of 8.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.740
Some of you might wonder why I don't solve these factorials?

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.100
That's because in the exams in all probability,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.720
your answer choices will also be in factorial form,

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.640
especially when the answer is huge!

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.260
And I'm sure you can solve these pretty easily

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.520
to get the numerical value.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.560
That's not the important part.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.160
The important part is understanding how we got this.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.060
But wait, we have the answers for each of these two cases.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.640
Boys and girls.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.180
What's the answer to the question?

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.880
The question asks us to find the number of ways,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.900
in which we can select three boys and three girls

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.900
out of ten boys and eight girls.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.440
So do we add these two or multiply them?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.680
The question gives us the answer.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.220
Three boys and three girls!

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.260
'And' always implies multiplication.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.600
So we multiply these two, and this becomes our final answer.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.460
If you solve this, you should get the answer

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.020
as six thousand seven hundred and twenty.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.720
Six thousand seven hundred and twenty ways

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.920
in which we can select three boys

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.820
and three girls from ten boys and eight girls.

